    "created_at": "2026-06-09T13:46:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-06-09T13:46:41Z",
    "body": "Indeed, a big reason to use `disclosure-open` is that it changes appropriately for `writing-mode`.  It also has the advantage (over Unicode characters) that we're not at the mercy of font availability and variation, or needing to ship an appropriate font with the browser.  (So far I think our experiences of using Unicode in this sort of context is quite limited although I think not quite zero.)  In Chrome at least, `disclosure-open` has [custom drawing code](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/paint/text_fragment_painter.cc;l=226-273;drc=001b5bdc78051d96a1646c791c5baac2a1b86734).  (Gecko's code is [at least writing-mode-aware](https://searchfox.org/firefox-main/rev/13c921de977264c9684ce23b0bfc46578babda18/layout/style/CounterStyleManager.cpp#832-853) though I'm not sure if there's custom drawing code; there used to be custom drawing code used only when used as a list item but not as a counter that was [removed](https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D111692); I don't immediately see any replacement that covers the counter case (which is now the only case).)\n\nI presume the suggestion for `content: <list-style-type>` is a suggestion to add a new CSS feature to support this?",
