    "created_at": "2026-06-09T16:38:19Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-06-09T16:38:19Z",
    "body": "Is the sole use-case for this making it look slightly less hacky to reuse the disclosure counters? Are there any other counters that are meaningfully usable here? \n\nThe counter-style system *in general* is a little weird to invoke here. We only have a handful of builtin styles that are totally static and don't care about the counter value. I wouldn't try to be generic here, honestly. If we want a more straightforward \"use the same glyph\" syntax, we could just add the specific keywords for those built-ins to 'content' (rather than allowing any counter-style name): disc, circle, square, disclosure-open, disclosure-closed.\n\nBut my larger question is indeed still just - can this just be solved by using a slightly more obvious counter name and a comment in the source, rather than inventing a new syntax? Or is it worth adding this syntax to solve an author issue outside of the HTML stylesheet itself?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
